WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.980
The Watcher Dispatch. From Toledo, Ohio, the essays of Ralph Koschnitzke on discernment,

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.960
leadership, and faith. Today's reading was written on September 17, 2026.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.220
See? There's something I've been noticing about the reaction to AI, especially when it

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:21.360
comes to music. What starts as a conversation about technology can quickly become a conversation

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.660
about creativity, authenticity, spirituality, and even our own sense of importance.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.420
That tells me the argument may not really be about the machine as much as we think it

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.620
is. I understand why people are cautious.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.840
Music is personal. It resonates with people.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:42.700
It carries memories, emotions, worship, heartbreak, and pieces of our lives that don't

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.860
always have words. When something new enters that space, it can feel like something human

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:52.240
is being replaced. But there's a difference between replacing the human and changing

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.060
the tools the human has available. I've played music for most of my life, and AI didn't

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.380
give me those years. It didn't give me the memories behind a song or the reason I wanted

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.860
to make it. It gives me another way to take an idea that is already in my head and work

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.860
with it. The computer can generate possibilities, but a human being still has to decide

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:16.420
what matters, what doesn't, what sounds right, what says something, and what gets thrown

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:21.560
in the trash. That's why I keep coming back to the idea that AI is a mirror, an advanced

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.160
one. What we bring to the tool has a lot to do with what we get back.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.360
Give it nothing but garbage, and it can produce some remarkably polished garbage.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.980
Bring experience, curiosity, musical taste, imagination, and something worth saying,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.840
and suddenly the same tool becomes useful in a completely different way.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.500
We've seen this happen with music technology before.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.180
Microphones, recording studios, electric instruments, synthesizers, drum machines, and

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.600
computers all change the creative process.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.540
Every generation has had its version of that isn't real music.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.200
Eventually, the technology becomes normal, and we forget that somebody once thought it

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.380
was cheating. AI is different in some important ways, and there are legitimate questions

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.700
about ownership, artists, originality, and how the technology is being used.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.060
Those questions deserve honest answers, but the mere presence of AI doesn't tell us what

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.260
is happening creatively. We still have to look at the person using it, and what they're

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.500
actually trying to accomplish. The same thing applies to faith.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.520
I don't believe AI has a soul, and I don't confuse a machine with God, but I also don't

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.160
think we should assume that God is limited by the tools we personally approve of.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.940
Scripture gives us the rather memorable example of God using a donkey to get someone's

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.380
attention. The donkey didn't become God because God used it.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.560
It was still a donkey. The instrument wasn't the point.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.680
That distinction matters. Sometimes our reaction to new technology is discernment.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.280
Sometimes it is legitimate concern. But sometimes it can also expose something inside

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.960
us that we'd rather not examine. When a new tool threatens something we've spent years

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.700
becoming good at, it can threaten more than a skill.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.380
It can threaten our identity. We like being the person who knows how to do something.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.160
We like having something that makes us valuable, unique, or needed.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.760
Then a new tool comes along and suddenly does part of what we spent years learning.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.420
It's pretty easy to become defensive about that.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.740
Even if we don't realize that's what's happening.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.840
That's where the mirror gets uncomfortable.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.140
The real issue isn't whether a computer can make a sound.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.800
The interesting part is what happens inside us when something comes along that challenges

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.060
the way we've defined creativity, authenticity, or our own importance.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.560
Because if my worth depends on being the best musician, the most creative person, the

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.000
person with the most knowledge, or the person who can do something a machine can't, sooner

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.860
or later something is going to come along and shake that foundation.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:49.340
A brief word before we finish. If this writing serves you, you can support the work and

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.960
follow along. Find Ralph on Facebook and Flogging Mars on TikTok and YouTube.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.860
The full archive lives at the Watcher Dispatch online, where a support link waits at

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.500
the top of the page. Thank you, truly. Now, back to the reading.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.040
Our worth has to be bigger than our abilities.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.580
That's one reason I keep coming back to Christ.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.480
He doesn't measure people by their usefulness, their talent, their resume, or their ability

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:18.140
to outperform somebody else. He goes straight past all that and deals with the heart.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.660
He calls us to truth, relationship, and a life that isn't built around proving ourselves.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.520
Technology will keep changing. The tools will keep getting better.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:32.100
Some of those changes will be good. Some will create problems and some will probably

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.840
make us uncomfortable. But underneath all of it, people will still be people looking

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.160
for meaning, connection, truth, and a reason to believe their lives matter.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.800
Maybe that's what we should be paying attention to.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.820
Not whether the tool has a soul, but whether the person using it is allowing God to work

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:53.660
on theirs. The tool is a mirror. What we see in it depends a lot on what we bring to

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:58.020
it. And when Christ holds the mirror up to us, he doesn't do it to leave us staring at

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.580
our flaws. He shows us the truth so we can stop performing, stop pretending, and find

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.140
the way, the truth, and the life in him.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:11.460
Ralph K. You have been listening to the Watcher Dispatch, the writing of Ralph Koschnitzke,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:17.400
the rock dog poet of Toledo, Ohio. The complete archive of his articles is open to read online.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.140
We watch so others can wake.

